}}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="container">

<!-- HEADER -->
<div class="report-header">
    <h1>Global Airbnb Market Comparison</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">Pricing, Supply &amp; Guest Demand Across 7 Major Cities &mdash; A cross-market analysis of 50,000 Airbnb listings examining pricing dynamics, room-type distribution, host concentration, and guest engagement patterns.</div>
    <div class="meta">Data: Inside Airbnb (scraped {R['scrape_date']}) &bull; Analysis date: April 2026 &bull; {fmt(R['total_listings'])} listings &bull; {fmt(R['total_hosts'])} unique hosts</div>
</div>

<!-- TOC -->
<div class="section">
    <h2>Contents</h2>
    <div class="toc">
        <a href="#market-overview"><span>01</span> Global Market Overview</a>
        <a href="#pricing-analysis"><span>02</span> Pricing Dynamics Across Cities</a>
        <a href="#supply-structure"><span>03</span> Supply Structure &amp; Host Concentration</a>
        <a href="#guest-demand"><span>04</span> Guest Demand &amp; Review Activity</a>
        <a href="#availability"><span>05</span> Availability &amp; Booking Constraints</a>
        <a href="#takeaways"><span>06</span> Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</a>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- DATA CAVEATS -->
<div class="caveat">
    <strong>Data Notes:</strong>
    The source dataset (50,000 rows) covers <strong>7 of the 10 originally specified cities</strong>. New York, Tokyo, and Los Angeles are not present in the sample provided. Additionally: <strong>Paris and Sydney have no price data</strong> (100% null in the price column), so they are excluded from all pricing analyses. <strong>Bangkok prices appear to be in Thai Baht</strong> (median ฿1,400 vs. $131&ndash;$222 elsewhere), likely reflecting local currency rather than USD normalization. Bangkok is included in price charts with a THB flag for transparency. All non-price metrics (room mix, host concentration, reviews, availability) are available for all 7 cities.
</div>

<!-- KPI CARDS -->
<div class="kpi-grid">
    {build_kpi_cards()}
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: MARKET OVERVIEW -->
<div class="section" id="market-overview">
    <h2>1. Global Market Overview</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">London and Paris dominate the dataset in raw listing volume, together accounting for over 61% of all listings. The remaining five cities range from nearly 6,400 listings (Rome) down to 1,770 (Amsterdam).</p>

    <h3>Listings by City</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box full">
            <canvas id="chartListingsByCity" height="80"></canvas>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Market Summary Table</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>City</th>
                <th class="num">Listings</th>
                <th class="num">Share</th>
                <th class="num">Median Price</th>
                <th class="num">Entire Home %</th>
                <th class="num">Private Room %</th>
                <th class="num">Professional Host %</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {build_market_overview_table()}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Room Type Distribution by City</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box full">
            <canvas id="chartRoomType" height="80"></canvas>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: PRICING ANALYSIS -->
<div class="section" id="pricing-analysis">
    <h2>2. Pricing Dynamics Across Cities</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Pricing analysis is limited to the 5 cities with available price data. Bangkok figures are in Thai Baht (THB) and are displayed separately for accuracy. Among USD-priced markets, Amsterdam commands the highest median nightly rate ($222), while Rome offers the most affordable at $131.</p>

    <div class="caveat">
        <strong>Coverage:</strong> Paris ({fmt(R['price_missing']['Paris'])} listings) and Sydney ({fmt(R['price_missing']['Sydney'])} listings) have no price data and are excluded. Among remaining cities, price coverage ranges from 81% (Bangkok) to 65% (London). Amsterdam has 56% coverage.
    </div>

    <h3>Median Nightly Price by City</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box">
            <div class="chart-title">USD-Priced Markets</div>
            <canvas id="chartMedianPrice" height="200"></canvas>
        </div>
        <div class="chart-box">
            <div class="chart-title">Price Distribution (Box Plot Approximation)</div>
            <canvas id="chartPriceRange" height="200"></canvas>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Price by City and Room Type</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Room Type</th>
                <th class="num">Median</th>
                <th class="num">Mean</th>
                <th class="num">Listings (n)</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {build_price_table_by_room()}
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <h3>Price Percentiles (USD Markets)</h3>
    <div class="insight-grid">'''

# Add price percentile cards for USD cities
usd_cities = ['Amsterdam', 'London', 'Barcelona', 'Rome']
for city in usd_cities:
    pp = R['price_percentiles'][city]
    report_html += f'''
        <div class="insight-card">
            <h4>{city}</h4>
            <div class="val">${fmt(pp["50%"])}</div>
            <div class="detail">25th: ${fmt(pp["25%"])} &bull; 75th: ${fmt(pp["75%"])} &bull; 90th: ${fmt(pp["90%"])}</div>
            <div class="detail">Range: ${fmt(pp["min"])}&ndash;${fmt(pp["max"])} &bull; n={fmt(pp["count"])}</div>
        </div>'''

report_html += f'''
    </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: SUPPLY STRUCTURE -->
<div class="section" id="supply-structure">
    <h2>3. Supply Structure &amp; Host Concentration</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Professional host share &mdash; defined as hosts with 5 or more listings &mdash; varies dramatically across cities. Bangkok (60.4%) and Barcelona (58.3%) have the highest concentration of professional-operated listings, while Amsterdam (6.8%) has the lowest, likely reflecting strict short-term rental regulation.</p>

    <h3>Professional Host Share by City (5+ Listings)</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box">
            <canvas id="chartProfHost" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
        <div class="chart-box">
            <div class="chart-title">Entire Home/Apt Share</div>
            <canvas id="chartEntireHome" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Host Portfolio Distribution</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>City</th>
                <th class="num">1 Listing</th>
                <th class="num">2 Listings</th>
                <th class="num">3&ndash;4 Listings</th>
                <th class="num">5+ Listings</th>
                <th class="num">5+ Share</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>'''

# sort by professional share descending
prof_sorted = sorted(R['host_distribution'].items(), key=lambda x: x[1]['5+_pct'], reverse=True)
for city, d in prof_sorted:
    total = d['1 listing'] + d['2 listings'] + d['3-4 listings'] + d['5+ listings']
    report_html += f'''<tr>
        <td><span class="city-dot" style="background:{COLORS[city]}"></span>{city}</td>
        <td class="num">{fmt(d['1 listing'])} ({pct(d['1 listing']/total*100)})</td>
        <td class="num">{fmt(d['2 listings'])} ({pct(d['2 listings']/total*100)})</td>
        <td class="num">{fmt(d['3-4 listings'])} ({pct(d['3-4 listings']/total*100)})</td>
        <td class="num">{fmt(d['5+ listings'])} ({pct(d['5+ listings']/total*100)})</td>
        <td class="num"><strong>{pct(d['5+_pct'])}</strong></td>
    </tr>'''

report_html += f'''
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: GUEST DEMAND -->
<div class="section" id="guest-demand">
    <h2>4. Guest Demand &amp; Review Activity</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Review metrics serve as proxies for guest demand intensity. Rome leads in average reviews per month (1.77), suggesting the strongest sustained booking activity, while Bangkok lags at 0.96. Review recency &mdash; the share of listings with at least one review in the past 12 months &mdash; highlights Rome and Sydney as the most actively booked markets.</p>

    <h3>Average Reviews per Month by City</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box">
            <div class="chart-title">Avg Reviews/Month</div>
            <canvas id="chartReviews" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
        <div class="chart-box">
            <div class="chart-title">Review Recency (% with review in last 12 months)</div>
            <canvas id="chartRecency" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Demand Metrics Table</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>City</th>
                <th class="num">Avg Reviews/Mo</th>
                <th class="num">Median Reviews/Mo</th>
                <th class="num">Avg Total Reviews</th>
                <th class="num">Avg Reviews (LTM)</th>
                <th class="num">Active Listings %</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>'''

review_sorted = sorted(R['reviews_by_city'].items(), key=lambda x: x[1]['avg_reviews_per_month'], reverse=True)
for city, d in review_sorted:
    recency = R['review_recency_pct'][city]
    report_html += f'''<tr>
        <td><span class="city-dot" style="background:{COLORS[city]}"></span>{city}</td>
        <td class="num">{d['avg_reviews_per_month']:.2f}</td>
        <td class="num">{d['median_reviews_per_month']:.2f}</td>
        <td class="num">{d['avg_total_reviews']:.1f}</td>
        <td class="num">{d['avg_reviews_ltm']:.1f}</td>
        <td class="num">{pct(recency)}</td>
    </tr>'''

report_html += f'''
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 5: AVAILABILITY & BOOKING CONSTRAINTS -->
<div class="section" id="availability">
    <h2>5. Availability &amp; Booking Constraints</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Availability and minimum-night requirements reveal regulatory footprints and host strategies. Amsterdam&rsquo;s extremely low median availability (18 days) reflects its strict short-term rental cap. Paris&rsquo;s high average minimum nights (43.9) signals the "bail mobilit&eacute;" (mobility lease) framework affecting a substantial portion of its market. Barcelona stands out with 38.5% of listings requiring 31+ nights, suggesting significant long-term rental regulation.</p>

    <h3>Average Availability (Days/Year)</h3>
    <div class="chart-grid">
        <div class="chart-box">
            <div class="chart-title">Mean Availability (days/365)</div>
            <canvas id="chartAvailability" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
        <div class="chart-box">
            <div class="chart-title">Minimum Night Requirements</div>
            <canvas id="chartMinNights" height="220"></canvas>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Availability &amp; Minimum Nights Table</h3>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>City</th>
                <th class="num">Avg Availability</th>
                <th class="num">Median Availability</th>
                <th class="num">Avg Min Nights</th>
                <th class="num">Median Min Nights</th>
                <th class="num">1 Night OK</th>
                <th class="num">31+ Nights</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>'''

for city in CITIES:
    ad = R['availability_by_city'][city]
    mn = R['min_nights_dist'][city]
    report_html += f'''<tr>
        <td><span class="city-dot" style="background:{COLORS[city]}"></span>{city}</td>
        <td class="num">{ad['avg_availability']:.0f} days</td>
        <td class="num">{ad['median_availability']:.0f} days</td>
        <td class="num">{ad['avg_min_nights']:.1f}</td>
        <td class="num">{ad['median_min_nights']:.0f}</td>
        <td class="num">{pct(mn['1 night'])}</td>
        <td class="num">{pct(mn['31+ nights'])}</td>
    </tr>'''

report_html += f'''
        </tbody>
    </table>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 6: KEY TAKEAWAYS -->
<div class="section" id="takeaways">
    <h2>6. Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">The following findings synthesize the cross-market analysis into actionable insights for investors, operators, and policymakers.</p>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>1. Amsterdam is the premium USD market.</strong> With a median nightly rate of $222 and the highest entire-home share (82.8%), Amsterdam offers strong revenue per listing. Yet its extremely low professional host share (6.8%) and median availability of just 18 days/year point to aggressive regulatory constraints that cap scale opportunities.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>2. Bangkok and Barcelona are dominated by professional operators.</strong> Over 58% of listings in both cities are managed by hosts with 5+ properties. In Bangkok, 60.4% of supply is professionally managed. Investors entering these markets face established, multi-property competitors and potential regulatory tightening.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>3. Rome delivers the strongest guest demand signal.</strong> With 1.77 average reviews per month and 73.4% of listings receiving a review in the past year, Rome shows the highest sustained booking activity. Its median price ($131) is the most affordable among priced markets, suggesting a volume-driven market.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>4. Paris is a highly commercialized but regulation-constrained market.</strong> An 88.2% entire-home share &mdash; the highest of any city &mdash; signals deep commercialization. Yet the average minimum-night requirement of 43.9 nights and 12.6% of listings requiring 31+ nights reflect France&rsquo;s &ldquo;bail mobilit&eacute;&rdquo; framework, which pushes many listings toward medium-term stays.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>5. London is the scale play with moderate pricing.</strong> The largest market (16,674 listings, 33.3% of the dataset) offers a median price of $135 and a relatively balanced mix of entire homes (64.7%) and private rooms (35.0%). Professional host share (32.2%) is moderate, leaving room for new entrants.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>6. Sydney has high professional concentration despite limited data.</strong> With 42.3% professional host share and 79.0% entire-home listings, Sydney&rsquo;s supply structure leans commercial. Its review recency (73.4%) matches Rome as the highest, suggesting strong active demand &mdash; but the absence of price data in this dataset limits investment analysis.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>7. Regulatory signals are readable in the data.</strong> Amsterdam&rsquo;s 18-day median availability, Barcelona&rsquo;s 38.5% long-stay requirement, and Paris&rsquo;s 44-night average minimum stay all point to cities where regulation materially shapes the market. Investors should factor regulatory trajectory into market-entry decisions.
    </div>

    <div class="takeaway">
        <strong>8. Price data gaps require caution.</strong> Paris and Sydney &mdash; two of the seven markets &mdash; have zero price data in this dataset. Bangkok prices are in Thai Baht, not USD. Any cross-market pricing conclusions must be qualified to the 4 cities with clean, comparable USD pricing (Amsterdam, London, Barcelona, Rome).
    </div>
</div>

